---
import Article from "../components/article.astro";
import Banner from "../components/banner.astro";
import { EnableConlangBuilderButton } from "../components/conlang-builder/enable-button";
import Heading from "../components/heading.astro";
import { MatchSuffixes } from "../components/inflection-and-derivation/match-suffixes";
import Paragraph from "../components/paragraph.astro";
import UnlockBanner from "../components/unlock-banner.astro";
import { WaitUntil } from "../components/wait-until";
import SectionLayout from "../layouts/section.astro";
---

<SectionLayout
  title="Inflection and derivation"
  position="3"
  page="inflection-and-derivation"
  progress={1}
  previousPath="/morphemes"
  nextPath="/allomorphy"
>
  <Article
    page="inflection-and-derivation"
    progress={1}
    concepts={["affixation", "inflection", "derivation"]}
    previousPath="/morphemes"
    nextPath="/allomorphy"
  >
    <Heading>Is all word formation the same?</Heading>

    <Paragraph>
      Let’s look at the free morpheme “teach”. If we attach the suffix “-er”, we
      get “teacher”. If we attach the suffix “-es”, we get “teaches”.
    </Paragraph>

    <Paragraph>Are these two suffixes doing the same kind of work?</Paragraph>

    <MatchSuffixes client:load />

    <WaitUntil client:idle page="inflection-and-derivation" progress={1}>
      <Paragraph>
        As you can see, affixes serve different purposes depending on how they
        interact with the base. This brings us to two major categories of
        affixation: <b>derivation</b> and <b>inflection</b>.
      </Paragraph>

      <Paragraph>
        When an affix fundamentally changes the core meaning or the grammatical
        category (e.g. noun, adjective, adverb, etc.) of the base, this process
        is called <b>derivation</b>. Turning the <i>verb</i> “teach” into the
        <i>noun</i> “teacher” is a derivational process.
      </Paragraph>

      <Paragraph>
        Conversely, when an affix attaches to a base merely to express a
        grammatical feature—such as tense (past/present/future) or quantity
        (single/dual/multiple)—without changing the word’s underlying meaning or
        category, it is called <b>inflection</b>. Adding “-es” to “teach” simply
        gives us the third-person singular present tense of the exact same verb.
      </Paragraph>

      <Banner>
        <p>
          Now that you have seen how word parts can change meaning, try it in
          your own language. Start with meaningful parts (morphemes), then
          combine them to make words. You can explore examples or create your
          own.
        </p>

        <p>
          The English toolkit now also tells you whether the morphemes of a word
          are serving an inflectional or derivational purpose.
        </p>
      </Banner>
      <UnlockBanner>
        <p>
          Start with a meaningful part, then combine parts into your first word.
        </p>
        <EnableConlangBuilderButton client:load />
      </UnlockBanner>
    </WaitUntil>
  </Article>
</SectionLayout>
